{"id": "eb788d4e-af1c-4fff-91a1-9e10b24af0ef", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:29:19.229594", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0d012364-facb-4b26-acdc-b62677c2104a", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-d84d9ba7-30a8-4d2d-8f22-99b4539eb90f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-d84d9ba7-30a8-4d2d-8f22-99b4539eb90f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Propose experimental-design approach for QuaRot on LLaMa-2\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Repository observations\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. No `quarot/`, no LLaMa-2 shards/json, no evaluation scripts, no notes.\\n- Python 3.12.3 present, system Python. No project deps installed, `which` not on PATH, `nvidia-smi` absent.\\n- 24 CPU threads, ~125 GiB RAM, no GPU detected. Network is disabled.\\n- Implication: any concrete execution of the design must happen in a different environment than this one; this environment can only produce the experiment plan.\\n\\n## Proposed experimental-design approach\\n\\n### 1. Model variants\\n- Primary: LLaMa-2 7B and LLaMa-2 13B. Both fit and are reproducible end-to-end on a single high-end GPU.\\n- Optional extension: LLaMa-2 70B, only if multi-GPU or 80GB-class memory plus CPU offload is granted; otherwise out of scope to avoid pretending a measurement we cannot honor.\\n- Hold the Meta-released revision constant (no fine-tunes) so FP16 vs W4A4 reflects the quantization effect, not a checkpoint difference.\\n\\n### 2. Precision / quantization conditions (paired within model)\\nPer model, three conditions (same calibration data, same code path):\\n- FP16 baseline: unrotated FP16 weights, FP16 activations, FP16 KV-cache. Control.\\n- W4A16 reference: weight-only 4-bit (RTN or GPTQ matching what QuaRot uses pre-rotation), FP16 activations. Intermediate, isolates the weight-quant contribution.\\n- QuaRot end-to-end W4A4: Hadamard-rotated weights and activations quantized to 4-bit, with the paper's learned per-channel / per-head clipping and outlier handling; KV-cache kept FP16 (the QuaRot default unless the researcher wants a +KV4 fourth cell).\\n\\n### 3. Workload definitions (prefill cleanly separated from decoding)\\n- Prefill-only (TTFT). Prompt lengths {256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192} tokens, max_new_tokens=1 so decoding contributes negligibly. Metric: TTFT captured via cuda.Event around the prefill phase.\\n- Decode-only (TPOT and tokens/s). Fixed prefill context (e.g. 512 tokens), generation lengths {64, 256, 512}. Metric: TPOT = (end-to-end - TTFT) / N, plus aggregate tokens/s.\\n- Long-context sweep. Same prefill cells, extended to 8k+ to make activation-bandwidth differences visible; truncate to LLaMa-2's 4k trained window or use RoPE extension unmodified.\\n- Mixed (realistic serving) workload. Smaller Cartesian product of prompt_len x gen_len so total latency and aggregate tokens/s can be reported jointly.\\n\\n### 4. Datasets / inputs\\n- Prefill prompts: WikiText-2 raw test split (natural language, no contamination from train/calibration); a held-out C4 sample as a secondary diversity check.\\n- Calibration: WikiText-2 train, several hundred sequences of fixed length, used strictly for rotation and clipping search, never for evaluation. Mirrors the QuaRot paper's default and keeps quality probes clean.\\n- Quality probes (guardrails, not the core performance question):\\n  - WikiText-2 test perplexity.\\n  - A small LM-Eval-Harness subset: HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande (zero-shot), to bound accuracy drift under W4A4.\\n\\n### 5. Measurement protocol\\n- Batch sizes {1, 4, 8, optionally 16 if VRAM allows}: exposes both interactive and small-batch-throughput regimes.\\n- Warmup: 3-5 untimed calls per cell to amortize kernel/JIT and allocator state.\\n- Timed runs: at least 30 measurements per (model x condition x batch x length) cell; report median, p95, and IQR.\\n- Counters of interest: TTFT (ms), TPOT (ms/token), aggregate tokens/s, peak GPU memory (allocated and reserved via torch.cuda), decode-only wall-time. Optional torch.profiler on one representative cell for kernel breakdown.\\n- Determinism: fixed prompt IDs across conditions; fixed seeds for sampling; identical tokenization; identical kernel stack across conditions; same generated length per cell.\\n\\n### 6. Analysis plan\\n- Paired comparisons. For each request ID, compute delta = T_quant - T_fp16 and analyze the paired distribution (Wilcoxon signed-rank, paired t-test, sign test as robustness check).\\n- Effect size. Report relative slowdown = T_quant / T_fp16 - 1, plus Cohen's d on log-latency.\\n- Decomposition. Tabulate prefill (TTFT) and decode (TPOT, tokens/s) effects independently before any end-to-end number so the phases are not conflated.\\n- Sensitivity. TTFT vs prompt length; TPOT vs context length and vs batch size; quality delta vs condition.\\n- Cross-model. 7B vs 13B (and 70B if available): does the relative benefit of W4A4 scale with model size / memory pressure.\\n- Quality guardrail. delta perplexity and delta accuracy on the LM-Eval subset, reported with the same paired framing but flagged as guardrail, not the main answer.\\n\\n## Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### Assumptions (must hold for the design to be valid)\\n- A1. Hardware: a single high-end GPU class (NVIDIA A100 80GB or H100), CUDA 12.x, with enough VRAM for the largest cell (13B W4A4 with KV-cache FP16 plus activations buffer).\\n- A2. Faithful QuaRot recipe: Hadamard rotation, per-channel/head clipping search, GPTQ-style weight quantization on rotated weights, and the small set of outlier channels kept in higher precision are reproduced as in the paper. Any deviation shifts the result and must be documented.\\n- A3. LLaMa-2 weights are accessible under Meta's license.\\n- A4. KV-cache stays FP16 by default in QuaRot, so W4A4 does not silently include KV quantization (subject to U1).\\n- A5. Identical kernel stack across conditions (FlashAttention-2 or SDPA), so quantization is what is being compared, not kernel choice.\\n- A6. Calibration data is WikiText-2 train, fixed sequence count, and is not reused for evaluation.\\n- A7. This design is the deliverable; the bubblewrap is not expected to execute the experiment.\\n- A8. Hypothesized directions and magnitudes (H1-H4 below) are guesses from prior published characterizations, not measurements under this task.\\n\\n### Uncertainties / open items to resolve before the final design\\n- U1. KV-cache quantization: should +KV4 be a fourth cell, or out of scope? My default is out of scope, mentioned as an extension. Needs confirmation.\\n- U2. End-to-end W4A4 perimeter: the residual outlier channels are kept in higher precision. Align to what the paper calls W4A4 and document the small FP16 share as a qualifier.\\n- U3. Canonical decoding metric: TPOT (steady-state, single-token) versus aggregate tokens/s. Report both, with TPOT primary for the decoding impact claim.\\n- U4. 70B scope: drop 70B if compute is tight rather than running it at a degraded protocol.\\n- U5. Environment gap: bubblewrap here cannot host the measured run; the design presumes a separate GPU-equipped environment.\\n- U6. Eval subset size and identity are negotiable; perplexity alone may be sufficient as the quality guardrail.\\n\\n### Hypothesized direction and magnitude (hypothesis only, not measured)\\n- H1. Prefill under W4A4: small or neutral effect at short prompts (kernel-launch and quantization overhead can wipe out bandwidth savings); modest positive effect at long prompts because activation bandwidth drops. Order-of-magnitude expectation: 0-15% relative TTFT change at 7B for prompts >= 2k tokens; 13B likely benefits slightly more due to higher memory pressure.\\n- H2. Decoding under W4A4: clearly positive on memory-bound regimes (long context, larger batch); aggregate throughput plausibly ~1.5x-3x FP16; TPOT reduction in the same ballpark.\\n- H3. W4A16 reference: smaller version of H2 (small but positive decoding effect, near-neutral prefill).\\n- H4. Quality: delta perplexity likely sub-1-point; small LM-Eval subset will be noisy and may sit inside that noise.\\n\\n### Proposed meaningful-impact thresholds (awaiting confirmation)\\n- T1. Prefill: |delta TTFT| >= 5% relative to FP16 is meaningful; below that is kernel / noise floor.\\n- T2. Decode: |delta TPOT| >= 10% meaningful overall, with a tighter >= 5% bar in clearly memory-bound cells (long context, batch >= 4).\\n- T3. Memory: >= 2x peak-memory reduction vs FP16 is the meaningful bar for the W4A4 claim.\\n\\n### Decisions awaiting researcher input before I finalize\\n1. KV-cache quantization policy (U1): include +KV4 or keep out of scope.\\n2. 70B inclusion (U4): in scope with stated multi-GPU plan, or dropped.\\n3. Impact thresholds T1/T2/T3: confirm or replace.\\n4. W4A16 intermediate cell: keep as a clean attributing reference, or drop to reduce measurement cost.\\n5. LM-Eval subset: confirm the proposed tasks or specify alternatives; perplexity-only is an option.\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection results from this turn:\\n\\n- `ls -la /workspace` shows a single 405-byte file `task.json` owned by uid 1776737281; the directory has no other entries.\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`; `find -type d` returns only `/workspace`.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` confirms the public task question and the documents.track.md note \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"; datasets is empty.\\n- `python3 --version` reports 3.12.3; `nproc` reports 24; `free -h` shows ~125 GiB total RAM; no `nvidia-smi` binary on PATH (which itself is also absent), and no `nvidia*` device entries appear, consistent with the task descriptor's `gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- Uname shows `Linux d33eaef-lcedt 7.0.0-38-generic ... x86_64`, matching a sandbox/bubblewrap.\\n\\nReproduction commands:\\n  ls -la /workspace\\n  find /workspace -maxdepth 3\\n  cat /workspace/task.json\\n  python3 --version && nproc && free -h\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":\"U1. +KV4 cell inclusion vs keeping KV-cache as FP16 by default.</item>\\n<item>U2. How strictly to define \\\"end-to-end 4-bit\\\" given the paper's small set of outlier channels kept in higher precision.</item>\\n<item>U3. Primary decoding metric: TPOT vs aggregate tokens/s.</item>\\n<item>U4. Whether 70B is in scope given the single-GPU assumption.</item>\\n<item>U5. Whether the proposed meaningful-impact thresholds (5% TTFT, 10% TPOT with 5% memory-bound bar, 2x memory) are accepted or need adjustment.</item>\\n<item>U6. LM-Eval-Harness subset identity and size, or whether perplexity-only is sufficient as the quality guardrail.</item>\\n<item>U7. Confirmation that the W4A16 intermediate cell is wanted as an attributing reference, given the extra measurement cost.</item>\\n<item>U8. Environment gap: bubblewrap cannot host the actual run; the design presumes a separate GPU-equipped execution environment.\",\"$text\":\"A1. Single high-end GPU class (A100 80GB or H100), CUDA 12.x, sufficient VRAM for the largest planned cell.</item>\\n<parameter name=\\\"uncertainties\\\">\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-86748f87-4fa0-4101-a1a9-b9e9ca433ab2", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
