decoding contributes negligibly. Metric: TTFT captured via cuda.Event around the prefill phase.\n- Decode-only (TPOT and tokens/s). Fixed prefill context (e.g. 512 tokens), generation lengths {64, 256, 512}. Metric: TPOT = (end-to-end - TTFT) / N, plus aggregate tokens/s.\n- Long-context sweep. Same prefill cells, extended to 8k+ to make activation-bandwidth differences visible; truncate to LLaMa-2's 4k trained window or use RoPE extension unmodified.\n- Mixed (realistic serving) workload. Smaller Cartesian product of prompt_len x gen_len so total latency and aggregate tokens/s can be reported jointly.\n\n### 4. Datasets / inputs\n- Prefill prompts: WikiText-2 raw test split (natural language, no contamination from train/calibration); a held-out C4 sample as a secondary diversity check.\n- Calibration: WikiText-2 train, several hundred sequences of fixed length, used strictly for rotation and clipping search, never for evaluation. Mirrors the QuaRot paper's default and keeps quality probes clean.\n- Quality probes (guardrails, not the core performance question):\n  - WikiText-2 test perplexity.\n  - A small LM-Eval-Harness subset: HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande (zero-shot), to bound accuracy drift under W4A4.\n\n### 5. Measurement protocol\n- Batch sizes {1, 4, 8, optionally 16 if VRAM allows}: exposes both interactive and small-batch-throughput regimes.\n- Warmup: 3-5 untimed calls per cell to amortize kernel/JIT and allocator state.\n- Timed runs: at least 30 measurements per (model x condition x batch x length) cell; report median, p95, and IQR.\n- Counters of interest: TTFT (ms), TPOT (ms/token), aggregate tokens/s, peak GPU memory (allocated and reserved via torch.cuda), decode-only wall-time. Optional torch.profiler on one representative cell for kernel breakdown.\n- Determinism: fixed prompt IDs across conditions; fixed seeds for sampling; identical tokenization; identical kernel stack across conditions; same generated length per cell.\n\n### 6. Analysis plan\n- Paired comparisons. For each request ID, compute delta = T_quant - T_fp16 and analyze the paired distribution (Wilcoxon signed-rank, paired t-test, sign test as robustness check).\n- Effect size. Report relative slowdown = T_quant / T_fp16 - 1, plus Cohen's d on log-latency.\n- Decomposition. Tabulate prefill (TTFT) and decode (TPOT, tokens/s) effects independently before any end-to-end number so the phases are not conflated.\n- Sensitivity. TTFT vs prompt length; TPOT vs context length and vs batch size; quality delta vs condition.\n- Cross-model. 7B vs 13B (and 70B if available): does the relative benefit of W4A4 scale with model size / memory pressure.\n- Quality guardrail. delta perplexity and delta accuracy on the LM-Eval subset, reported with the same paired framing but flagged as guardrail, not the main answer.\n\n## Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\n\n### Assumptions that must hold for the design to be valid\n- A1. Hardware: a single high-end GPU class (NVIDIA A100 80GB or H100), CUDA 12.x, enough VRAM for the largest cell (13B W4A4 with KV-cache FP16 plus activations buffer).\n- A2. Faithful QuaRot recipe: Hadamard rotation, learned per-channel/head clipping search, GPTQ-style weight quantization on rotated weights, and the paper's small set of outlier channels kept in higher precision are reproduced as published. Any deviation shifts results and must be documented.\n- A3. LLaMa-2 weights are accessible under Meta's license.\n- A4. KV-cache stays FP16 by default in QuaRot, so W4A4 does not silently include KV quantization.\n- A5. Identical kernel stack across conditions (FlashAttention-2 or SDPA), so quantization is what is being compared, not kernel choice.\n- A6. Calibration data is WikiText-2 train, fixed sequence count, and is not reused for evaluation.\n- A7. This design document is the deliverable; the bubblewrap is not expected to execute the experiment.\n- A8. The hypothesized direction/magnitude numbers (H1-H4 below) are guesses from prior published characterizations, not measurements under this task.\n\n### Uncertainties / decisions awaiting researcher input\n- U1. KV-cache quantization: include a +KV4 cell, or keep KV-cache at FP16 (the paper's default) and mention 4-bit KV cache only as an extension?\n- U2. End-to-end 4-bit perimeter: the residual outlier channels are kept in higher precision. Confirm the design reports what the paper calls W4A4 and documents the small FP16 outlier share as a qualifier.\n- U3. Canonical decoding metric: TPOT (steady-state, single-token) vs aggregate tokens/s. Default in this design is to report both, with TPOT primary for the decoding impact claim.\n- U4. 70B scope: keep in scope with a stated multi-GPU / offload plan, or drop to focus on 7B+13B cleanly.\n- U5. Environment gap: bubblewrap here cannot host the measured run; the design presumes a separate GPU-equipped execution environment.\n- U6. Eval subset size and identity are negotiable; perplexity alone may be sufficient as the quality guardrail.\n- U7. W4A16 intermediate cell: keep it as a clean attributing reference (weight vs activation shortcut attribution), or drop it to reduce measurement cost.\n\n### Hypothesized direction and magnitude (hypothesis only, not measured under this task)\n- H1. Prefill under W4A4: small or neutral effect at short prompts (kernel-launch and quantization overhead can wipe out bandwidth savings); modest positive effect at long prompts because activation bandwidth drops. Order-of-magnitude expectation: 0-15% relative TTFT change at 7B for prompts >= 2k tokens; 13B likely benefits slightly more due to higher memory pressure.\n- H2. Decoding under W4A4: clearly positive on memory-bound regimes (long context, larger batch); aggregate throughput plausibly ~1.5x-3x FP16; TPOT reduction in the same ballpark.\n- H3. W4A16 reference: smaller version of H2 (small but positive decoding effect, near-neutral prefill).\n- H4. Quality: delta perplexity likely sub-1-point; small LM-Eval subset may sit inside that noise.\n\n### Proposed meaningful-impact thresholds (awaiting confirmation)\n- T1. Prefill: |delta TTFT| >= 5% relative to FP16 is meaningful; below that is kernel / noise floor.\n- T2. Decode: |delta TPOT| >= 10% meaningful overall, with a tighter >= 5% bar in clearly memory-bound cells (long context, batch >= 4).\n- T3. Memory: >= 2x peak-memory reduction vs FP16 is the meaningful bar for the W4A4 memory-claim.\n\n### Decisions before I finalize the design\n1. KV-cache quantization policy (U1): include +KV4 or keep out of scope.\n2. 70B inclusion (U4): in scope with stated multi-GPU plan, or dropped.\n3. Impact thresholds T1/T2/T3: confirm or replace.\n4. W4A16 cell (U7): keep as a clean attributing reference, or drop.\n5. LM-Eval subset (U6): confirm the proposed tasks or specify alternatives; perplexity-only is an option.", "evidence": "Inspection results from this turn:\n\n- `ls -la /workspace` shows a single 405-byte file `task.json` owned by uid 1776737281; the directory has no other entries.\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`; `find -type d` returns only `/workspace`.\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` confirms the public task question and the documents.track.md note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"; `datasets` is empty.\n- `python3 --version` reports 3.12.3; `nproc` reports 24; `free -h` shows ~125 GiB total RAM; no `nvidia-smi` binary on PATH (which itself is also absent), and no `nvidia*` device entries appear, consistent with the task descriptor's `gpu_enabled: false`.\n- Uname shows `Linux d33eaef-lcedt 7.0.0-38-generic ... x86_64`, matching a sandbox/bubblewrap.\n\nReproduction commands:\n  ls -la /workspace\n  find /workspace -maxdepth 3\n  cat /workspace/task.json\n  python3 --version && nproc && free -h", "assumptions": ["A1. Single high-end GPU class (A100 80GB or H100), CUDA 12.x, enough VRAM for the largest cell.</item>\n<item>A2. Faithful QuaRo