{"id": "7273bcbc-5a1c-47d4-aa98-8f4259bc985f", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:30:10.102767", "source": "user", "parent_id": "b553e89e-88a3-465a-8894-e577c9c393e0", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Authorize the main work: produce the final experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility) per the submission contract. Resolve the open decisions as follows so the final design is self-contained and does not re-litigate them; document each choice inside the design with a one-line justification. (U1) KV-cache stays FP16 in the primary matrix, matching the QuaRot default; add a brief 'out-of-scope extension' subsection noting W4A4+KV4 as a follow-up rather than a measured cell. (U2) Adopt the paper's published W4A4 definition and flag the small FP16 outlier share as a documented qualifier on every headline number. (U3) TPOT is the primary decoding metric; aggregate tokens/s is the secondary, reported alongside it, never in place of it. (U4) Drop 70B from primary scope to honor the single high-end GPU assumption; record it as a future-extension footnote with the multi-GPU/offload conditions that would be required. (U6) Keep the small LM-Eval subset as a quality guardrail (HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, zero-shot), with WikiText-2 perplexity as the primary quality anchor; report these alongside performance, clearly labeled guardrail. (U7) Keep the W4A16 cell as an attributing reference; the modest additional cost is justified by the weight-vs-activation attribution it enables. Thresholds T1/T2/T3 (5% prefill, 10% decode / 5% in memory-bound cells, 2x peak memory) stand as proposed. Additionally: structure the final design with explicit sections for Objective, Models, Conditions, Workloads (prefill-only, decode-only, long-context sweep, mixed), Datasets and Splits (with calibration/evaluation separation), Measurement Protocol (hardware, warmup, runs, counters, determinism controls), Analysis Plan (paired tests, effect sizes, decomposition across phases, sensitivity sweeps, cross-model), Quality Guardrails, Feasibility (hardware budget, disk/RAM, expected wall time, threats to validity), and the documented out-of-scope extensions. Where the prior turn stated a hypothesis (H1-H4), restate it as an explicit prior hypothesis in the design, clearly labeled as a prior, not as a measurement. Do not claim the experiment was executed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "55e2f808-b1af-45d7-8eed-e72f673f2ae2", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:32:05.677259", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7273bcbc-5a1c-47d4-aa98-8f4259bc985f", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-5b5ae866-db35-4030-a98e-f9e1d247e9fd", "tool_call": {"id": "call-5b5ae866-db35-4030-a98e-f9e1d247e9fd", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Submit final experiment design for QuaRot W4A4 vs FP16 on LLaMa-2\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"## Final Experiment Design — QuaRot W4A4 vs FP16 on LLaMa-2 prefill and decoding\\n\\n### 1. Objective\\nQuantify how QuaRot's end-to-end 4-bit (W4A4) quantization changes the prefill and decoding performance of LLaMa-2 relative to an FP16 baseline, at 7B and 13B, with 5%/10%/2× meaningful-impact thresholds for prefill, decode, and memory respectively. Prefill and decoding are judged on their own metrics (TTFT and TPOT) and then composed into an end-to-end number; the headline split is always prefill vs decode, never blended.\\n\\n### 2. Models\\n- LLaMa-2 7B (primary).\\n- LLaMa-2 13B (primary).\\n- LLaMa-2 70B: out of primary scope, recorded as a future-extension footnote (§12).\\n\\nModel revisions are pinned to the Meta-released checkpoints (no fine-tunes), keeping the comparison one of quantization, not of checkpoint.\\n\\n### 3. Conditions (paired within model)\\n| ID | Weights | Activations | KV-cache | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 | FP16 | FP16 | FP16 | Baseline control. |\\n| C1 | 4-bit (GPTQ-on-rotated path or RTN matching QuaRot pre-rotation) | FP16 | FP16 | Attributing reference (weight-only). |\\n| C2 | QuaRot: 4-bit on rotated weights + learned clipping | 4-bit on rotated activations + paper's clipping | FP16 | Main condition of interest. |\\n\\nDocumented qualifier carried on every C2 headline number (per U2 / Q1): the paper retains a small set of outlier channels in higher precision; the cell reports the share explicitly so the headline claim has the same scope as the paper.\\n\\nOne-line justifications:\\n- C0 is the only behaviorally clean control.\\n- C1 isolates weight-only quantization so a C2 vs C1 contrast attributes the residual effect to activation 4-bit.\\n- C2 is the condition the question is about.\\n\\n### 4. Workloads (prefill cleanly separated from decoding)\\n4.1 Prefill-only — prompt lengths {256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192} tokens; max_new_tokens=1, greedy decoding so prefill dominates cell time; TTFT measured via cuda.Event at the prefill-to-decode boundary.\\n4.2 Decode-only — fixed prefill context = 512 tokens; generation lengths {64, 256, 512}; TPOT = (end-to-end − TTFT) / N; aggregate tokens/s reported as the secondary metric (per U3).\\n4.3 Long-context sweep — prompt lengths {4096, 8192, 16384}-after-truncation within LLaMa-2's 4096 trained window; same TTFT gating with one sanity decode cell at one batch setting.\\n4.4 Mixed serving cell — small Cartesian {256, 1024, 4096} prompt × {64, 256} generation × batch {1, 4, 8} (16 if VRAM allows); identical prompts/seed across conditions.\\n\\n### 5. Datasets and Splits\\n5.1 Calibration — WikiText-2 train (~256–512 sequences at natural length). Used only for C1's GPTQ path and C2's rotation + clipping search. Calibrated once per (model, condition) and cached.\\n5.2 Prefill evaluation prompts — WikiText-2 raw test, deduplicated, sliced at token boundaries to the target length. Strictly disjoint from calibration. ~32 prompt IDs per length cell, fixed across conditions. Optional secondary: ~32 C4-streaming prompts.\\n5.3 Decoding evaluation prompts — WikiText-2 test, fixed 512-token context, same prompt IDs across conditions.\\n5.4 Quality guardrails — WikiText-2 test perplexity as the primary quality anchor; lm-eval-harness zero-shot subset (HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande) as the generative-quality probe. Both are labeled guardrails.\\n\\nCalibration/evaluation separation is enforced by an explicit file-list diff check (§9.5).\\n\\n### 6. Measurement Protocol\\n6.1 Hardware — Single high-end GPU (NVIDIA A100 80GB or H100). CUDA 12.x. One GPU per cell, conditions sequential. Disk <200 GB local; RAM 64 GB+ recommended.\\n6.2 Software — PyTorch with FlashAttention-2 or SDPA, identical kernel stack across C0/C1/C2 (hard requirement). Transformers + the published QuaRot reference implementation for rotation, learned clipping, GPTQ-on-rotated-weights, and outlier retention. Both are required at execution and absent here.\\n6.3 Warmup — 5 untimed calls per cell, two including tokenization so KV-cache allocation is steady-state.\\n6.4 Timed runs — 30 timed measurements per cell per (model, condition, batch, length); median, p95, IQR.\\n6.5 Counters — TTFT (ms), TPOT (ms/token), aggregate tokens/s, peak allocated GPU memory (MiB), peak reserved GPU memory (MiB), prefill-only wall-time, decode-only wall-time, end-to-end wall-time. Optional torch.profiler on one representative cell per (model, condition) for kernel attribution.\\n6.6 Determinism — torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True), CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG set, fixed seeds, greedy decoding, identical tokenization, identical kernel stack, identical batch composition across conditions.\\n6.7 Pairing — same prompt IDs across C0/C1/C2 at each length/batch cell — every statistical test is paired by request id.\\n\\n### 7. Analysis Plan\\n7.1 Paired tests — per request id, Δ = T_quant − T_fp16 across 30 measurements; primary test = paired Wilcoxon signed-rank; paired t-test on log-latency and a sign test as robustness checks.\\n7.2 Effect sizes — relative change rel = T_quant / T_fp16 − 1, median + IQR per cell; Cohen's d on log-latency (paired); peak memory ratio peak_mem_fp16 / peak_mem_quant (meaningful ≥2× per T3).\\n7.3 Decomposition across phases — always tabulate prefill (TTFT) and decode (TPOT, tokens/s) independently before any end-to-end number; end-to-end = TTFT + N·TPOT, both components broken out.\\n7.4 Sensitivity sweeps — TTFT vs prompt length; TPOT vs generation length and vs prompt context length; aggregate tokens/s vs batch size at a fixed context length; per-condition markers for the T1/T2 thresholds.\\n7.5 Cross-model — 7B vs 13B: stack Δ-TTFT and Δ-TPOT as a function of prompt length and batch; checks the prior that W4A4's benefit scales with model size.\\n7.6 Quality guardrail analysis — Δ-perplexity on WikiText-2 test; Δ-accuracy per lm-eval task with the paper-relevant subset as a separate panel. Guardrails are labeled and not folded into the performance verdict.\\n\\n### 8. Quality Guardrails\\n- WikiText-2 test perplexity reported for C0, C1, C2; expects sub-1-point Δ for C2 vs C0 on the paper's regime.\\n- lm-eval-harness zero-shot subset (HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande) for C0, C1, C2.\\n- Guardrails are reported alongside performance; a guardrail breach is reported as such rather than as a reason to suppress performance numbers.\\n\\n### 9. Feasibility\\n9.1 Hardware budget — 7B FP16 ~14 GiB; 13B FP16 ~26 GiB; 70B excluded (§12). W4A4 reduces weights and in-flight activations; KV-cache FP16 dominates transient VRAM. 80 GB-class fits both 7B and 13B at the largest planned length and batch.\\n9.2 Wall time (prior estimate, not measured) — 24–72 GPU-hours per model on A100, ~5–15× faster on H100. Depends on the workload matrix chosen at execution.\\n9.3 Disk and RAM — ~50 GB for 7B artifacts (FP16 + W4A16 + W4A4 + intermediate scale tensors); ~100 GB for 13B. 64 GB+ RAM recommended; 125 GB has headroom.\\n9.4 Threats to validity — (i) implementation drift from paper — pin recipe version; (ii) kernel-stack differences — lock FA-2/SDPA across conditions; (iii) calibration leakage — file-list diff in §9.5; (iv) single-checkpoint confounds — Meta-revision pin; (v) hardware variance — run conditions on the same physical GPU at contiguous times plus one verification rerun per representative cell; (vi) KV-cache quantization interactions — fixed FP16 per U1; +KV4 out of scope (§12); (vii) sampling determinism — greedy decoding, fixed seeds, deterministic algorithms.\\n9.5 Feasibility checks needed at execution (not run in this design) — (a) confirm /workspace contains expected checkpoint paths after staging; (b) confirm CUDA version + FlashAttention-2 import; (c) confirm a single FP16 forward pass for 7B and 13B at the largest prompt length fits the target VRAM envelope; (d) file-list diff between wt2.train.ids and wt2.test.ids with empty intersection; (e) smoke TTFT/TPOT measurement with small N confirming timings have non-trivial variance and are > 0.\\n\\n### 10. Restated prior hypotheses (explicit priors; not measurements)\\nThese restate the priors from the approved approach so the analysis can be checked against them. None of these is a measurement under this design.\\n- Prior H1 (prefill under W4A4): small or neutral effect at short prompts; modest positive effect at long prompts as activation bandwidth falls. Magnitude prior: 0–15% relative TTFT change at 7B for prompts ≥ 2k tokens; 13B may benefit slightly more.\\n- Prior H2 (decoding under W4A4): clearly positive on memory-bound regimes (long context, larger batch). Magnitude prior: aggregate throughput ~1.5×–3× FP16; TPOT reduction in the same ballpark.\\n- Prior H3 (W4A16 reference): smaller version of H2; small positive decoding effect, near-neutral prefill.\\n- Prior H4 (quality): Δ-perplexity sub-1 point on WikiText-2 test; lm-eval zero-shot subset will be noisy.\\n\\n### 11. Meaningful-impact thresholds (fixed by prior approval)\\n- T1 Prefill: |Δ TTFT| ≥ 5% relative to FP16 = meaningful; below that, kernel / noise floor.\\n- T2 Decode: |Δ TPOT| ≥ 10% overall = meaningful; tighter ≥ 5% bar in clearly memory-bound cells (long context, batch ≥ 4).\\n- T3 Memory: ≥ 2× peak-memory reduction vs FP16 = meaningful W4A4 memory claim.\\n\\nSub-threshold deltas are reported but labeled within kernel/noise floor.\\n\\n### 12. Out-of-scope extensions (documented, not executed)\\n- A. +KV4 cell — QuaRot's experimental 4-bit KV cache as an additional W4A4+KV4 condition. Out of primary scope per U1; not measured here.\\n- B. 70B — would require tensor-parallel across 2–4 A100/H100 80GB or an explicit offload strategy. Primary scope is 7B + 13B.\\n- C. Speculative decoding, paged KV, chunked prefill — orthogonal engineering choices; isolated by a fixed kernel stack per §6.2.\\n- D. Continuous-batching serving stacks (vLLM, TGI) — separate study, not in question's scope.\\n- E. Other model families (LLaMA-3, Mistral) — explicitly out of scope.\\n\\n### 13. Documented qualifiers (carried on every headline number)\\n- Q1. \\\"W4A4\\\" matches the paper's definition, including the small set of outlier channels in higher precision; the cell reports the percentage of channels and parameters that are truly 4-bit.\\n- Q2. C1 is pinned to GPTQ-on-rotated-or-RTN as executed, recorded in the artifact manifest.\\n- Q3. Calibration data is fixed to WikiText-2 train; any deviation must be reported.\\n- Q4. Quality numbers are guardrails, not the answer.\\n\\n### 14. Self-contained summary of resolved decisions\\n- U1 → KV-cache FP16 in primary matrix; +KV4 listed in §12.\\n- U2 → paper's W4A4 definition; FP16 outlier share is a documented qualifier (Q1).\\n- U3 → TPOT primary, aggregate tokens/s secondary, both reported (§4.2, §6.5, §7.3).\\n- U4 → 70B dropped from primary, listed in §12.\\n- U6 → WikiText-2 perplexity primary quality anchor; lm-eval zero-shot subset (HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA, WinoGrande) as guardrail (§5.4, §8).\\n- U7 → W4A16 cell kept as an attributing reference (§3).\\n- T1/T2/T3 → stand as proposed (§11).\\n\\nDesign only; no experiment was performed under this task.\",\"evidence\":\"Workspace inspection results from this session:\\n\\n- `ls -la /workspace` returned only `task.json` (405 bytes) owned by uid 1776737281; no other files or directories.\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` and `find -type d` both confirmed `/workspace` is otherwise empty.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` confirmed the public task question and the documents.track.md note \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"; `datasets` is empty.\\n- `python3 --version` reports 3.12.3 (system Python). No project dependencies are installed; `which` is not on PATH, `nvidia-smi` is absent.\\n- `nproc` = 24; `free -h` reports ~125 GiB total RAM; no nvidia* device entries appear, consistent with `gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- Uname: `Linux d33eaef-lcedt 7.0.0-38-generic ... x86_64` (sandbox/bubblewrap).\\n\\nRepository observations carried into the design as feasibility constraints:\\n- No QuaRot source present → QuaRot reference implementation is a required execution dependency.\\n- No LLaMa-2 checkpoints/config shards present → Meta-licensed checkpoints are a required execution dependency.\\n- No evaluation scripts present → the dataset/split/check protocol defined in §5/§9.5 becomes the canonical spec for any execution-side script.\\n- No GPU present → the design presumes a separate GPU-equipped environment for §6.1; all measurements are deferred.\\n- Wall-time and feasibility numbers in §9.2 are priors from prior published characterizations of similar setups, explicitly labeled as such and not measured here.\\n\\nReproduction commands for the inspection:\\n  ls -la /workspace\\n  find /workspace -maxdepth 3\\n  cat /workspace/task.json\\n  python3 --version && nproc && free -h\",\"assumptions\":[\"A1. Single high-end GPU class (NVIDIA A100 80GB or H100), CUDA 12.x, with VRAM headroom for the largest planned (model, condition, batch, length) cell.</item>\\n<item>A2. Faithful QuaRot recipe: Hadamard rotation, learned per-channel/head clipping, GPTQ-on-rotated-weights, and the paper's small set of outlier channels in higher precision are reproduced as published; the recipe version is pinned in the artifact manifest.</item>\\n<item>A3. LLaMa-2 weights are accessible under Meta's license at execution time.</item>\\n<item>A4. KV-cache stays FP16 by default in QuaRot; W4A4 in the primary matrix does not silently include KV quantization (per U1 resolution).</item>\\n<item>A5. Identical kernel stack (FlashAttention-2 or SDPA) is used across C0/C1/C2 so the comparison reflects quantization, not kernel choice.</item>\\n<item>A6. Calibration on WikiText-2 train only, with an enforced file-list disjointness check against evaluation sets.</item>\\n<item>A7. This design document is the deliverable; the bubblewrap is not expected to execute the experiment.</item>\\n<item>A8. Prior hypotheses H1-H4 are priors taken from prior published characterizations of similar quantizations and decoding regimes; they are restated in §10 to anchor the analysis, not to pre-commit any conclusion.</item>\"],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"U10. lm-eval subset sample size at execution time may be small; the design keeps it as a guardrail so a noisy guardrail is not over-interpreted.</item>\",\"$text\":\"U9. Software pin at execution time: torch / transformers / FlashAttention-2 versions are not yet decided; the design only requires the kernel stack be constant across C0/C1/C2.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U8. Hardware pin at execution time (A100 vs H100) is not yet decided; the design gates on a single high-end GPU class but does not commit to a vendor SKU.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U5. Environment gap: bubblewrap cannot host the measured run; the design presumes a separate GPU-equipped execution environment (closed at handoff time; open at execution time).</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f8d185ac-0dc2-4cd0-a470-5490c398b165", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
